d side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +\\beta =7\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{14}{5},\\beta =\\frac{7}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{14}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$" 13194139536008 true] [17592186229603 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536008 true] [17592186229603 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n$3\\frac{dy}{dx} - 9y = 6\\sin (2x)$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{3x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.$y(x) = Ae^{3x} + ($\n{{field-1}}\n$) \\cdot \\cos (2x) + ($\n{{field-2}}\n$) \\cdot \\sin (2x)$\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"solving-an-initial-value-problem\"></a>" 13194139536008 false] [17592186229603 1100 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-9y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{3x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" 13194139536008 true] [17592186229603 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta -9\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta -9\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -9\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{13},\\beta =-\\frac{6}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{6}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+(-\\frac{4}{13})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-\\frac{6}{13})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$" 13194139536008 true] [17592186319049 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379215 13194139536008 true] [17592186319099 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379242 13194139536008 true] [17592186379210 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379211 13194139536008 true] [17592186379210 1089 "q-332622" 13194139536008 true] [17592186379210 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536008 true] [17592186379210 1100 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536008 true] [17592186379210 1113 #uuid "b5abf4eb-a16d-59fe-ae4c-da3f3734dace" 13194139536008 true] [17592186379210 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivati